WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.847
Eli Bendersky's notes

00:00:01.659 --> 00:00:02.460
on the discrete

00:00:02.272 --> 00:00:03.533
Fourier transform

00:00:04.062 --> 00:00:05.889
Eli Bendersky continues

00:00:05.701 --> 00:00:06.927
his signal-processing

00:00:06.739 --> 00:00:08.177
series by deriving

00:00:07.989 --> 00:00:09.109
the DFT for

00:00:08.921 --> 00:00:10.524
finite sampled signals

00:00:10.336 --> 00:00:11.526
from the discrete-time

00:00:11.338 --> 00:00:12.517
Fourier series

00:00:13.054 --> 00:00:14.103
Real signals are

00:00:13.915 --> 00:00:15.306
finite and discrete

00:00:15.389 --> 00:00:16.591
so the DTFT's

00:00:16.403 --> 00:00:17.712
continuous frequency

00:00:17.523 --> 00:00:18.796
function cannot be

00:00:18.608 --> 00:00:19.822
computed directly

00:00:19.962 --> 00:00:20.928
The post walks

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:21.494
through the math

00:00:21.306 --> 00:00:22.473
with worked examples

00:00:22.603 --> 00:00:23.746
following his earlier

00:00:23.558 --> 00:00:24.395
posts on the

00:00:24.207 --> 00:00:26.412
DTFS and DTFT

00:00:26.942 --> 00:00:28.498
First by convention

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:29.394
1 we define

00:00:29.513 --> 00:00:30.161
And then

00:00:30.444 --> 00:00:31.234
This is called

00:00:31.046 --> 00:00:32.142
the Discrete Fourier

00:00:31.954 --> 00:00:33.085
Transform of

00:00:33.606 --> 00:00:34.620
Full story on

00:00:34.431 --> 00:00:35.327
the dashboard
